# 1. What is CSS?

CSS stands for:

**Cascading Style Sheets**

CSS is used to control the **appearance and design** of HTML elements.

HTML = Structure  
CSS = Design  
JavaScript = Behavior

Example:

HTML:

    <h1>My Profile</h1>

CSS:

    h1 {
        color: blue;
    }

Output:

    My Profile
    ↑
    Blue color

---

# 2. CSS Syntax

Basic CSS syntax:

    selector {
        property: value;
    }

Example:

    h1 {
        color: blue;
        font-size: 30px;
    }

### Meaning

- `h1` → selector
- `color` → property
- `blue` → value
- `font-size` → property
- `30px` → value

Think:

    What do I want to style?
            ↓
         selector
            ↓
    What do I want to change?
            ↓
         property
            ↓
    What value do I want?
            ↓
          value

---

# 3. CSS Selectors

## Element Selector

Selects all elements of that type.

    p {
        color: purple;
    }

This affects every `<p>` element.

---

## Class Selector

A class starts with `.` in CSS.

HTML:

    <div class="card">
        My Profile
    </div>

CSS:

    .card {
        background-color: ivory;
    }

### Remember

HTML:

    class="card"

CSS:

    .card

---

## ID Selector

An ID starts with `#`.

HTML:

    <h1 id="title">My Website</h1>

CSS:

    #title {
        color: blue;
    }

---

# 4. Basic CSS Properties

## color

Changes text color.

    h1 {
        color: red;
    }

---

## background-color

Changes the background color.

    .card {
        background-color: ivory;
    }

---

## font-size

Changes text size.

    p {
        font-size: 20px;
    }

---

## text-align

Controls text alignment.

    h1 {
        text-align: center;
    }

Common values:

    left
    center
    right

---

# 5. Border

The `border` property creates a border around an element.

    border: 2px solid black;

Meaning:

    2px   → thickness
    solid → border style
    black → border color

Example:

    .box {
        border: 2px solid grey;
    }

---

# 6. Border Radius

`border-radius` makes corners rounded.

    border-radius: 15px;

Example:

    .card {
        border-radius: 30px;
    }

Higher value → more rounded corners.

---

# 7. Padding

Padding is the space **inside an element**, between the content and border.

    padding: 20px;

Think:

    ┌──────────────────────┐
    │      PADDING         │
    │   ┌──────────────┐   │
    │   │   CONTENT    │   │
    │   └──────────────┘   │
    │      PADDING         │
    └──────────────────────┘

Example:

    .card {
        padding: 30px 50px;
    }

Two-value shorthand:

    padding: 30px 50px;

means:

    Top/Bottom    = 30px
    Left/Right    = 50px

---

# 8. Margin

Margin is the space **outside an element**.

    margin: 20px;

Example:

    .card {
        margin: 20px auto;
    }

Two values:

    margin: 20px auto;

means:

    Top/Bottom = 20px
    Left/Right = auto

`auto` can be used to center a block element horizontally when it has a defined width.

---

# 9. Padding vs Margin

Very important:

    Padding → inside the element
    Margin  → outside the element

Easy memory trick:

    PADDING = Personal space inside
    MARGIN  = Space between elements

---

# 10. CSS Box Model

Every HTML element can be understood as a box.

    Content
       ↓
    Padding
       ↓
    Border
       ↓
    Margin

Diagram:

    ┌─────────────────────────────┐
    │           MARGIN            │
    │   ┌─────────────────────┐   │
    │   │       BORDER        │   │
    │   │  ┌───────────────┐  │   │
    │   │  │    PADDING    │  │   │
    │   │  │ ┌───────────┐ │  │   │
    │   │  │ │  CONTENT  │ │  │   │
    │   │  │ └───────────┘ │  │   │
    │   │  └───────────────┘  │   │
    │   └─────────────────────┘   │
    └─────────────────────────────┘

---

# 11. Width

Controls the width of an element.

    width: 400px;

Example:

    .card {
        width: 400px;
    }

---

# 12. box-sizing

A useful CSS property:

    box-sizing: border-box;

It makes the declared width include:

    content + padding + border

A common professional CSS reset is:

    * {
        box-sizing: border-box;
    }

This makes sizing easier to control.

---

# 13. Flexbox

Flexbox is used to arrange elements in a row or column.

To start Flexbox:

    display: flex;

Example:

    .container {
        display: flex;
    }

Without Flexbox:

    Java
    SQL
    HTML
    DSA

With Flexbox:

    Java   SQL   HTML   DSA

---

# 14. flex-direction

Controls the direction of Flexbox items.

## Row

    flex-direction: row;

Output:

    Java   SQL   HTML   DSA

Default direction is usually `row`.

## Column

    flex-direction: column;

Output:

    Java
      ↓
    SQL
      ↓
    HTML
      ↓
    DSA

---

# 15. Flexbox Axes

This is VERY important.

For:

    flex-direction: row;

The axes are:

    Main axis   → horizontal
    Cross axis  ↓ vertical

For:

    flex-direction: column;

The axes change:

    Main axis   ↓ vertical
    Cross axis  → horizontal

### Golden rule

    justify-content → Main axis
    align-items     → Cross axis

This rule remains true even when the direction changes.

---

# 16. justify-content

Controls the position of Flexbox items on the **main axis**.

Example:

    .container {
        display: flex;
        justify-content: center;
    }

For a row:

    Java   SQL   HTML   DSA
              ↑
            center

Common values:

    flex-start
    center
    flex-end
    space-between
    space-around
    space-evenly

---

# 17. align-items

Controls the position of Flexbox items on the **cross axis**.

Example:

    .container {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
    }

For a row:

    justify-content → horizontal
    align-items     → vertical

Remember:

    JUSTIFY = Main axis
    ALIGN   = Cross axis

---

# 18. gap

`gap` controls the space between Flexbox items.

    .container {
        display: flex;
        gap: 20px;
    }

Output:

    Java ←20px→ SQL ←20px→ HTML

### Important difference

    margin  → space around an element
    padding → space inside an element
    gap     → space between Flexbox/Grid items

---

# 19. flex-wrap

By default, Flexbox tries to keep items on one line.

    flex-wrap: nowrap;

If the screen becomes smaller, items can become crowded.

Use:

    flex-wrap: wrap;

Then items can move to another row.

Example:

    Java   SQL   HTML   CSS   JavaScript
    React  Python DSA   Git

### Remember

    nowrap → stay on one line
    wrap   → move to another line when needed

This is important for responsive websites.

---

# 20. box-shadow

Adds a shadow around an element.

Example:

    box-shadow: 0 4px 10px gray;

Meaning:

    0    → horizontal movement
    4px  → vertical movement
    10px → blur
    gray → shadow color

Example:

    .card {
        box-shadow: 0 4px 10px gray;
    }

This makes cards look more modern.

---

# 21. :hover

`:hover` applies CSS when the mouse moves over an element.

Example:

    .box:hover {
        background-color: lightskyblue;
    }

Normal:

    ┌─────────┐
    │  Java   │
    └─────────┘

Mouse over:

    ┌─────────┐
    │  Java   │  ← changes
    └─────────┘

---

# 22. transform: scale()

Used to make an element slightly bigger or smaller.

Example:

    transform: scale(1.05);

`1` = normal size

`1.05` = slightly bigger

Example:

    .box:hover {
        transform: scale(1.05);
    }

This gives a small interactive effect.

---

# 23. text-decoration

Used to add decorations to text.

Underline:

    text-decoration: underline;

No underline:

    text-decoration: none;

Strike through:

    text-decoration: line-through;

Example:

    .section-title {
        text-decoration: underline;
    }

---

# 24. Why Classes Are Useful

Suppose we have:

    <h2>Kothamekala Nikitha</h2>

and:

    <h2 class="section-title">My Skills</h2>

If we write:

    h2 {
        text-decoration: underline;
    }

BOTH headings get underlined.

Instead:

    .section-title {
        text-decoration: underline;
    }

Only `My Skills` gets the underline.

### Lesson

Use an element selector when you want to style many elements.

Use a class when you want specific elements to have special styling.

---

# 25. Internal CSS vs External CSS

## Internal CSS

CSS is written inside `<style>`.

    <style>
        h1 {
            color: blue;
        }
    </style>

Useful for small experiments.

---

## External CSS

CSS is placed in a separate file.

Folder:

    Day2/
    ├── index.html
    └── style.css

HTML:

    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">

CSS goes inside:

    style.css

### Why external CSS is better

- Cleaner HTML
- Easier maintenance
- Can reuse CSS
- Better organization
- Standard approach for real projects

---

# 26. Important External CSS Rule

In `index.html`:

    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">

In `style.css`:

    h1 {
        color: blue;
    }

DO NOT put:

    <style>
    </style>

inside `style.css`.

CSS file contains CSS only.

